Z||000000||Coupe'N||06-17-2001||03:22 PM||||Hey everyone. I see mustangs that run 90-10 shocks, WHAT ARE THOSE? I see fieros that run adjustable shocks, WHAT ARE THOSE? What kinda performance increase will I see over OEM shocks?<BR>ANY info on this matter is greatly appreciated!!!<P>Coupe'N outt!!!||||reg||6
Z||000001||theogre||06-17-2001||03:59 PM||||90-10 no idea. sounds like allot of money for a name....<P>adjustable... mainly expensive. not usually benifitial to street car. Makes them really stiff. Depending on which springs are on the car can be to stiff to drive comforatbly. Might even be unsafe with really stiff springs because the suspension won't be able to keep all the wheels on the ground on rough pavement.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||johnt671||06-17-2001||06:25 PM||||Aren't they drag racing shocks with 10% front and 90% rear to trasfer weight on launching?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Coupe'N||06-17-2001||09:17 PM||||yes john, they are drag shocks used for weight transfer. I thought someone here would know exactly how they work. We'll see...! Anyone?<P>Coupe'N outt!!!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Coupe'N||06-17-2001||10:16 PM||||Well, I found out this much from some mustang guys... 90/10 shock: the front goes up easy 10% and then settles down slow 90% (resistance). Now if only I can find out some namebrands that have these type of set ups.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick's Dad||06-17-2001||11:03 PM||||Most everything I've seen about 90/10's say, "NOT FOR STREET USE! ONLY FOR DRAG STRIP!"<P>Because of different rebound issues, the 90/10's can easily cause a loss of control on the street.<P>Adjustable shocks are streetable shocks that let you adjust (by way of a knob or a rachet) the firmness of the shock damping. You might want this for a smooth ride on the street, then dialed up to firm for autocrossing. Koni are the most popular of these.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||06-17-2001||11:20 PM||||90-10... If as described above, very simple actually. it's just a valve setting in the shock. The valve lets it go up easy but closes to make it come down slow. I expect they run something like that as a front shock. Would really only work for drag. AutoX would bite.<P>I think Koni is the only one that has an adjustable for Fiero. Unless you are rebuilding the entire suspension.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Coupe'N||06-17-2001||11:23 PM||||Thanks for the info Patrick's Dad. Do they have an application specifically for the fiero, or do I have to measure the OEM shocks first? I am swapping my 84 WS6 suspension into my 87 coupe, and would like it to handle the best it can. <BR>Thanks again for your help guys<P>Coupe'N outt!!!||||reg||6
Z||000008||lowCG||06-18-2001||11:03 PM||||You don't want those shocks,especially on a Fiero!||||reg||13
